  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Phụ âm · The last consonant: ng / ngh",
     "texto": [
      "**ng** and **ngh** sound the same: like the «ng» in «sing» (the nasal alone, without the «g»), and **at the start** of a word. It can be hard for English speakers, because in English a word never starts like that.",
      "Tip: say «singer» very slowly, stop at the «ng» before the «g» and start from there: **ngủ**, **ngày**.",
      "**ngh** only appears in front of **e, ê, i** (nghe, nghỉ); in other cases it is written **ng** (ngủ, ngày). It is only spelling: it sounds the same."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Nguyên âm · Vowels",
     "texto": [
      "**ao**: «ow», as in «how».",
      "**au**: like «ow» in «how», with a long a.",
      "**âu**: also «au», but shorter and with a closer a (almost «uh-oo»): **đâu** ≈ «dow».",
      "**eo**: a very open «e» plus «o»: «eh-oh».",
      "**êu**: «ay-oo» with a closed «e», shorter than **eo**.",
      "**ươ**: a glide from **ư** to **ơ** (in the south it sounds more even). It always has a consonant after it: **người**, **trường**.",
      "**uyê**: a glide from u to i to ê: «wee-eh» (in the south, more like «wee»). After it only **-n** or **-t** can come: **chuyện**, **tuyết**.",
      "**ao, au, âu, eo, êu** always end the syllable; **ươ** and **uyê** always take a final consonant."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
